\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Uniqueness is monotonicity}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{\citet{p. 64}{ermentrout2010mathematical} records that a unique
equilibrium for every applied current has never been rigorously
proved for the Hodgkin–Huxley equations.
\citet{p. 65}{ermentrout2010mathematical} says that this claim is
the monotonicity of the steady current of \ref{fnip-0076} for every
voltage, and that the monotonicity depends on the steady-state
functions, so a general account is unlikely. The example below
checks one closed interval.}

\p{Order an equilibrium with the voltage first. The
[Jacobian](fnip-0034) is then zero except on the diagonal, the
first row, and the first column.
\citet{pp. 64–65}{ermentrout2010mathematical} computes the
characteristic polynomial from that shape and calls the shape
mammillary. The other coordinates are #{n}, #{m}, and #{h} for
the Hodgkin–Huxley system, and the single coordinate #{n} for
Morris–Lecar.}
